WHAT WAS TAKEN. Two glyphs, held in one module.

`src/data/mapIcons.js` holds the artwork:

    security_camera — the surveillance-camera billboard, drawn by
                      `src/data/cctv.js`.

    fighter_jet     — `military=airfield`, drawn by
                      `src/data/militarySiteIcons.js`.

Temaki is the icon set of the OpenStreetMap iD editor and covers subjects no
interface icon set does; a wall-mounted surveillance camera is one of them, and
so is a combat aircraft. Material Symbols has neither: what `security_camera`
replaced was this project's own drawing, and what `fighter_jet` replaced was
Material's `flight` — an airliner, which is the right glyph for the civil
aerodrome pack next door and the wrong one for an air base.

WHY IT REPLACED THAT DRAWING. Not taste — a colour bug.

The old camera baked cyan into the artwork (`#75e7ff` strokes over dark fills)
while the layer tints the billboard to mark state: `#6be8ff` at rest, `#ffd97a`
for the ACTIVE camera. Cesium multiplies `billboard.color` into the texture, so
the selected camera rendered #75e7ff × #ffd97a = **#75c57a**, a green. White
artwork makes that multiply an identity and the active camera is amber, as the
layer always intended.

WHAT WAS CHANGED. No coordinate.

Only the `d` path strings are vendored, in Temaki's own space, NOT rescaled.
Temaki is not uniform about that space: `security_camera` is published in
`0 0 15 15` and `fighter_jet` in `0 0 48 48`. Each box is DECLARED
(`MAP_ICON_BOX` in `src/data/mapIcons.js`) rather than normalised, because
rescaling is a redraw and a redraw is no longer the artwork that was evaluated;
everything downstream — the padded viewBox, the halo width, the transform that
fits a silhouette into a 96-unit plate — is expressed as a ratio of that box.

Temaki publishes the camera as three sibling paths — a body, a wall bracket, and
a hood whose lens is a counter-wound subpath — and they are kept whole, in
order, and are never merged: merging them would fill the lens.

At render time each path is drawn TWICE by this project: once as a wide dark
stroke (a halo, so the glyph stays readable over pale terrain) and once as a
white fill (so Cesium's per-billboard colour multiply can tint it). The viewBox
is padded to `-1 -1 17 17` so that halo is not clipped — this glyph spans the
full published box, from x=0 to x=15. The path geometry itself is untouched, and
`src/data/mapIcons.test.mjs` pins it.
